# Keys everywhere (K1): the window worked without the mouse, on the notes `cursor.seed.ts` writes. The beds are
# found by their title and opened in the reader; the tree takes the keys, and its cursor starts from the row of
# the note its reader shows: that row unfolded by the cursor's own fold, the reader's reading line put on the
# first part of its page, and the cursor two rows further on. Two marks in the tree, never one for both: the
# cursor's row (an edge and a fill while its tile has the keys) and the open note's (a quiet fill). The reader's
# reading line is the bar beside its part, dim while the reader hasn't the keys, left where it was. Each title
# says its tile's number among the stops Tab goes round, and the row under the tiles says the keys of the tile
# that has them, from the key table. (No row is counted from an end of the tree: the notes a new outline starts
# with are written by the service in its own time, before or after the seed's.)
bar.open scope=search query="Four beds this year"
bar.pick n=1
tile.focus tile=tree
tree.fold open=true
reading.step by=1 tile=reader
list.step by=1
list.step by=1
